  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through next Tuesday) \nIssued at 1038 PM CDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nThe upper shortwave will continue to lift northeastward across the \nHigh Plains through the beginning of the extended period while a \nsecondary disturbance tracks northward through portions of eastern \nNM. As a result, the FA will remain positioned within a favorable \nsoutherly flow regime aloft, between the departing waves and an \namplifying ridge to the east. This set up will continue to support \nthe transport of subtropical moisture into the region, with PWATS \nremaining elevated and well above climatological standards. Generally \nranging from 1.5\" across the Caprock and up to 1.75\" off the Caprock \nthrough Friday. With several smaller perturbations embedded within \nthe flow, daily chances for showers and thunderstorms will persist \nthrough the end of the work week. The better potential for more \nwidespread precipitation looks to occur Thursday and Friday, \nparticularly during the late evening and overnight hours, as a more \norganized disturbance tracks near the region. An intensifying LLJ \nduring this timeframe should help enhance moisture transport and \nconvergence, while moist isentropic ascent around the 315K surface \nhelp to provide additional sources of lift and moisture for shower \nand thunderstorm development. Given the abundant moisture in place, \nlocally heavy rainfall will remain the primary concern, though \nstrong to marginally severe wind gusts cannot be ruled out within \nstronger storm cores. As well as small hail, especially if storms \nare able to grow upscale overnight, we could see a small risk for \nelevated hailers. Overall the severe potential remains limited, \nthough localized flooding could become an issue with the expectation \nof slow storm motions along with training storms. \n\nInto the weekend, the upper pattern will begin to shift as ridging \ngradually builds back across the region. This should allow the \ndeeper moisture to slowly retreat, with PWATs and low-level moisture \ntrending downward through the weekend. As a result, precipitation \nchances will decrease and temperatures will begin to climb back \nabove seasonal standards, leading to a slightly warmer and drier \npattern Saturday and Sunday. Though drier, we cannot rule out an \nisolated storm or two with any lingering moisture and passing \ndisturbances within the flow. Attention then turn towards next week, \nas ensemble guidance begins to show increasing potential for another \nupper level disturbance to dig southeastward into the Four Corners. \nTHis could eventually support renewed precipitation chances and \nanother round of cooler temperatures across the region. However, \nensemble spread remains fairly large at this time, with at least \nhalf the members maintaining highs in the 90s next week. Therefore, \nconfidence in the magnitude and timing of any potential cooldown \nremains low.",
